The Most Expensive Moment in Your Funnel

Your outbound did its job. The prospect replied. The meeting got booked. The calendar invite went out. Then the Account Executive walked into the call knowing nothing, and the prospect had to explain everything again.

That's the handoff moment. And it's where most deals lose momentum.

An SDR learns why the prospect replied. They know what problem got mentioned. They understand what made the prospect hesitate. But if none of that reaches the AE, the first call restarts from zero. The prospect feels like they're talking to a stranger. The warmth your outbound created disappears.

Signs Your Handoff Is Broken

Look for these patterns in your pipeline:

Prospects repeat themselves: They explain the same problem twice. Once to the SDR. Once to the AE. Each conversation feels like starting over.
AEs open with a generic pitch: They don't know which pain point got the reply, so they start with the full product tour instead of a conversation.
Show rates drop: Prospects book with one person and confirm with nobody. They quietly skip the call.
Blame goes both ways: AEs say the leads are weak. SDRs say the AEs don't follow up. Nobody looks at the handoff itself.
What a Good Handoff Note Includes

Before every meeting, the SDR should pass on a short, consistent brief. Five lines is enough. The goal is context, not a report.

Why they replied: The exact message or angle that got the response. Not a summary. The real reason.
The problem in their words: Quote what they said, not what you think they meant. This matters.
Fit and timing: Company size, role, the trigger that made them relevant, and any timeline they mentioned.
Objections so far: Anything they pushed back on or asked about. Pricing. Current vendors. Contract length. Any hesitation.
Who else is involved: Any colleagues they mentioned or copied in. This shapes the conversation.

That's it. Five pieces of information. When the AE has this, they walk in already briefed. The call moves forward instead of circling back.

Make the Transition Visible to the Prospect

The handoff shouldn't feel like a transfer to a call centre. Have the SDR introduce the AE by name in the confirmation email, with one line on why this person is the right one to talk to.

When the AE opens the call by referencing what the prospect already shared, it signals that your company listened. It says: you're not talking to a stranger. You're talking to someone who knows your situation.

That signal matters more than the script.

Close the Loop After the Call

Handoffs should work in both directions. After each meeting, the AE should tell the SDR whether the lead was a real fit and why. Not a scorecard. Just honest feedback.

That feedback sharpens targeting and messaging far faster than any monthly report. The SDR learns which angles actually convert. Which problems matter. Which companies are worth the time. The next campaign improves because the SDR knows what actually works.

